    "text": "NKU iGEM 2026 NKU iGEM 2026 - an early-warning biosensor concept asking whether engineered yeast could be designed to read nematode-derived ascaroside signals from soil. Home NKU-iGEM26 / SUBSURFACE The soil already knows. We are exploring whether engineered yeast can read nematode ascaroside clues as a possible upstream signal before visible root damage. Open the case Trace the design Case no. 26-001 ascaroside clue candidate GPCR under test The suspect: invisible. The signal: chemical. The proposed reader: engineered yeast, still a design hypothesis. proposed reader Minimal wiki map Six doors, one evidence trail. Each door opens one part of the investigation: the problem and its targets, the engineering path, what the records do and do not show, the human context, safety boundaries, and how credit is recorded. 01 Project Problem, targets, and why soil-borne chemical signals matter. 02 Engineering The design-build-test-learn path for the yeast reporter concept. 03 Results Documented observations, negative and inconclusive checks, limits, and validation gates. 04 Human Practices Engagement still to be documented, and the design questions it has to answer. 05 Safety Responsible boundaries for engineered yeast and any proposed soil-facing use. 06 Team & Attributions Contributions and outside help, recorded as each is confirmed. Mechanism, not a product claim A signal chain we can test, step by step. SUBSURFACE is an investigation rather than a device: literature-grounded ascaroside cues, receptor candidates, a yeast signalling chassis, and a reporter readout still to be chosen and tested. cue Nematode ascarosides Chemical trail in soil, treated as a literature-derived target input. receptor Heterologous GPCR candidate A candidate recognition layer; its ascr#18 response is untested and must be measured directly. chassis Engineered yeast pathway A synthetic-biology platform intended to couple receptor activation into a cellular signal, still under construction. readout Proposed reporter readout Unselected: the measurement layer where results and limits will be recorded. Case file stays open Follow the biology below the surface. The questions stay open: whether the candidate GPCR responds to ascr#18 at all, whether a rewired yeast pathway could carry that signal, and what a reporter would have to show to be trusted. Negative and inconclusive records are part of the trail. Read the design Meet the team",
